ILP64 gateway fix for MUMPS 5.3.5 MATLAB/mumpsmex.c
===================================================
Only needed on Linux, where DeHNSSo builds MUMPS with 64-bit integers
(-fdefault-integer-8 / -DINTSIZE64) because the factors of a 1M-DOF Jacobian
exceed 2^31 entries.

The stock gateway allocates these arrays as C `int` (4 bytes) while the ILP64
library writes MUMPS_INT (8 bytes) into them: a heap overflow that shows up as
"free(): invalid next size" when the instance is destroyed.

install_mumps_linux.sh applies this with sed. The lines are:

  line 334   MYMALLOC((dmumps_par->jcn),(int)netrue,int);
             ->  MYMALLOC((dmumps_par->jcn),(int)netrue,MUMPS_INT);

  line 335   MYMALLOC((dmumps_par->irn),(int)netrue,int);
             ->  MYMALLOC((dmumps_par->irn),(int)netrue,MUMPS_INT);

  line 416   EXTRACT_FROM_MATLAB_TOPTR(PERM_IN,(dmumps_par->perm_in),int,((int)n));
             ->  ...,MUMPS_INT,((int)n));

  line 431   EXTRACT_FROM_MATLAB_TOPTR(VAR_SCHUR,(dmumps_par->listvar_schur),int,...);
             ->  ...,MUMPS_INT,...);

irhs_sparse (line 503) is left as int: it is unused with a dense RHS, which is
all DeHNSSo sends.

64-bit nonzero counts (added 18 Sep 2026)
-----------------------------------------
The gateway also casts the nonzero count to C int: the array lengths
(int)netrue on lines 316-319, nz = (int)ne on line 339, and the loop counters
`int i,j,pos` on line 234. A Jacobian with more than 2^31 nonzeros therefore
fails with "Malloc failed in mumpsmex.c" (the length wraps negative). The
installer widens them: `mwSize i,j,pos`, `(size_t)netrue` in the three
MYMALLOCs, `(MUMPS_INT)netrue` for nz_alloc, `(MUMPS_INT)ne` for nz, and
`(MUMPS_INT)irn_in[j]` for the two row-index copies. MUMPS_INT is 8 bytes in
this build, so nz holds the count. First hit on a 2500x80 Chebyshev, N = 5
swept-plate Jacobian with 2.93e9 nonzeros.
